{
  "reference_class": "Independent daily coin-flip markets resolved by a random number generator (FairlyRandom), where YES = heads and NO = tails",
  "base_rate": "A fair coin lands heads about 50% of the time, so roughly 1 in 2 of these daily markets resolve YES. Over the 536 prior flips in this series, heads came up 278 times (51.9%) vs 258 tails (48.1%) — slightly above the expected 50%, but within ~1.7 standard deviations of the mean for n=536, so statistically indistinguishable from a fair coin.",
  "case_specific_adjustments": "Each flip is designed to be independent and random, so yesterday's tails result and any streak patterns provide no predictive information. The only case-specific evidence is the mild empirical excess of heads across 536 flips (278 vs 258). Applying Laplace's rule of succession gives (278+1)/(536+2) ≈ 51.9% heads, suggesting a possible tiny bias in the generator, though this is likely just noise. Offsetting this is the strong prior that @FairlyRandom is a genuinely fair RNG. Net adjustment: a negligible bump upward from 0.50.",
  "probability": 0.505
}

{
  "reference_class": "Probability that a major stock index (DJIA) touches a fixed level ~2.5-4% below its current price within a ~5-6 week window, including intraday touches. This is a barrier-touching event driven by index volatility, not an end-point forecast.",
  "base_rate": "DJIA daily volatility is ~1.0-1.1% (≈16-18% annualized), so over ~27-30 trading days the standard deviation of returns is ~5.5%. For a zero-drift random walk, the probability of touching a barrier d% below the start is roughly 2×Φ(−d/σ√T). For d ≈ 2.5-3.5%, this gives ~45-60%. Empirically, most calendar years contain several 3%+ pullbacks, so a randomly chosen 6-week window contains one roughly half the time, with wide regime variation (e.g., quiet 2017-like regimes: ~25-35%; volatile 2018/2020/2022-like regimes: 70%+). Baseline: about 5 in 10.",
  "adjustment": "Case-specific factors: (1) The index sits at/near record highs (52,000-53,000 in early July 2026), implying a strong bull run — momentum and positive drift modestly reduce the touch probability, but extended markets also carry fat-tail downside risk that raises it; (2) the barrier is only ~2.5-3% away if the index starts near the lower end of its range (52,000), pushing the probability toward ~60-70%, vs. ~45% if starting near 53,000; (3) the window includes late July/early August, which historically has produced episodic sharp corrections (2011, 2024 yen-carry unwind) but is on average a mildly positive, lower-volatility seasonal stretch; (4) resolution uses intraday lows, which modestly increases touch probability versus close-only monitoring, matching the continuous-barrier math. These factors roughly offset, leaving the estimate near the base rate, perhaps a touch above even money.",
  "probability": 0.54
}

{
  "reference_class": "Daily random coin-flip markets resolved by an automated RNG (@FairlyRandom) on Manifold; each day is an independent binary outcome (heads = YES, tails = NO).",
  "base_rate": "A truly fair coin resolves YES 50% of the time (1 in 2). Empirically, this series has produced 278 heads in 536 flips (≈51.9% heads), which is within ~0.9 standard deviations of 50% — consistent with a fair process plus ordinary variance.",
  "case_specific_adjustments": "Yesterday's result (tails) is irrelevant — flips are independent, so no gambler's-fallacy adjustment. The observed 278-258 heads surplus is not statistically significant, so it should only weakly move the estimate off 50%. Applying Bayesian shrinkage toward the strong prior that the RNG is fair (the 20-flip gap over 536 trials suggests at most a tiny true bias), the historical heads excess of ~1.9 points shrinks to roughly +1 point. Streak lengths (10 heads, 9 tails) are also consistent with fairness. Slight edge to YES, roughly 51%.",
  "probability": 0.51
}

{
  "reference_class": "NFL playing-rule change proposals that were formally proposed and failed to reach the 24-vote supermajority at an owners' meeting, then face re-consideration the following offseason. Examples include the sky-judge/officiating-replay proposals (repeated failures), the fourth-and-15 onside-kick alternative (failed multiple years, never passed), versus cases like expanded pass-interference review and the modernized kickoff (failed or stalled once, then passed within 1-2 years).",
  "base_rate": "Within this reference class, roughly 2 in 5 such re-proposed rule changes pass within the next year or two; safety-framed proposals with league-office backing pass more often (closer to 1 in 2), while proposals lacking injury data or competition-committee endorsement pass less often.",
  "case_specific_adjustments": "Points toward YES: the tush-push ban reportedly came close in the 2025 vote (roughly 22-10, just short of the 24 needed), suggesting only a couple of votes must flip; the issue has persisted for multiple offseasons, indicating durable owner interest; and the resolution criteria is broad (any rule prohibiting pushing in some circumstances, with any effective date, before Preseason 2026), capturing even a watered-down amendment. Points toward NO: the competition committee has repeatedly declined to endorse the ban; injury data has not supported the safety argument; the NFLPA has opposed it; more teams have adopted their own versions of the play, diluting the competitive-fairness motive; and the 24/32 supermajority threshold plus repeated failure suggests entrenched positions. Overall these roughly balance, with a slight lean toward another failed vote but with meaningful probability the league eventually legislates it away, as it has with other widely-debated plays.",
  "probability": 0.48
}

{
  "reference_class": "Adoption of a specific NFL playing-rule change at the league's spring owners meetings, where the same change was formally proposed in prior years but tabled rather than passed (e.g., the tush push / pushing-the-runner ban proposed by the Packers in 2024 and 2025)",
  "base_rate": "Roughly 2 in 10. Most NFL rule proposals require 24 of 32 owners (75%) to pass, and proposals that have been repeatedly tabled in prior years usually die or get tabled again the following year (e.g., the fourth-and-15 onside-kick alternative, expanded replay for penalties). A minority of persistent proposals do eventually pass after multi-year advocacy (e.g., the hip-drop tackle ban, overtime possession changes).",
  "adjustments": "Upward factors: the play has extraordinary salience because of the Eagles' sustained success (including a Super Bowl win) with it, owner frustration with its aesthetics persists, the 2025 tabling was explicitly framed as 'keep reviewing during the season' rather than a rejection on the merits, and a redrafted, narrower proposal (e.g., banning only pushing the quarterback) could attract votes the broad 2024-25 language lost. Downward factors: the ban was tabled in consecutive years without ever reaching a vote, the Eagles' lobbying and injury data have been effective, the competition committee has repeatedly declined to sponsor the change (committee-backed proposals pass far more often than team proposals), the loudest advocate (Packers president Mark Murphy) retired in 2025, and there was no strong injury evidence supporting a safety rationale. These roughly offset with a slight net nudge upward from the possibility of a re-scoped proposal and lingering momentum.",
  "probability": 0.28
}
